# ⭐ Every component writes its build output to its own build/ (since
# 2026-09-23 - docs/history.md), which the first line covers wherever it is:
# Verilator's objects, bench output, ROM builds, CMake builds, and machine_tb's
# frame captures. build-*/ is the root CMake builds' old home.
build/
build-*/
*.o
*.elf
*.bin
# ... except the boot ROM image, which is what the machine executes: a design
# output, tracked like the fitted .jed files. software/boot/README.md.
!software/boot/boot.bin
*.hex
*.map
*.lst
compile_commands.json
.cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Verilator output from before the build/ directories, and the archived demo's.
obj_dir/
obj_demo/

# Reference scans (datasheets, service manuals, schematics). Kept out of
# history: they are ~93 MB and are freely available from the vendors and from
# colorcomputerarchive.com. Each reference/ subdirectory has a README listing
# the expected files and what cites them, so a fresh clone can be refilled.
# The READMEs themselves are deliberately not covered by these patterns.
**/reference/**/*.pdf
**/reference/**/*.tiff
# (since 2026-09-23 every component has its own reference/, hence the leading
# **/ - and supplied inputs that are not ours join them: a photograph and the
# archived demo's modules)
**/reference/**/*.jpg
**/reference/**/*.mod

# The ATF15xx toolchain's Wine prefix, kept in the repository rather than in
# $HOME so that it survives a sandboxed session - hardware/tools/gal/prjbureau/
# extract-wincupl.sh rebuilds it from awincupl.exe.zip in about a minute. It is
# 1.3 GB and it contains Microchip's WinCUPL, which this project has no right to
# redistribute; CLAUDE.md's "The CPLD fitter and CUPL" section is the setup.
.wine_atf/

# Third-party binaries and source archives that this project has no right to
# redistribute (Apple ROM images, the CHM QuickDraw/MacPaint releases). Tracked
# until 2026-09-04; see reference/68k/README.md and docs/design-review.md Sys-M6.
**/reference/**/*.ROM
**/reference/**/*.rom
**/reference/**/*.zip
# ⛔ AND DISK IMAGES.  A retail game disk is not the MIT-licensed sources, and
# software/pcs/reference/pcs-source/README.md's rule is that the right to redistribute is the
# criterion - the same rule that purged Apple's ROM and the CHM archives on
# 2026-09-04.  pcsfile.py reads the tables out of these locally; nothing in the
# tree depends on them being present.
**/reference/**/*.dsk
**/reference/**/*.atr
**/reference/**/*.po
**/reference/**/*.woz

# Vendor installers. awincupl.exe.zip is Microchip's WinCUPL, which
# hardware/tools/gal/prjbureau/extract-wincupl.sh unpacks for the ATF15xx fitters and
# the CUPL compiler. Licensed, 20 MB, and freely downloadable - not ours to
# redistribute, and reference/README.md's rule for vendor blobs applies.
awincupl.exe.zip
*wincupl*.zip

# LWTOOLS and ToolShed, built by software/tools/fetch-nitros9-tools.sh, and the
# LWTOOLS source archive it builds from (GPL v3, not ours to redistribute).
.tools/
software/tools/lwtools-*.tar.gz

# rendered videos (software/nitros9/video/run-video.sh, software/archive/demo/tools/mkvideo.py):
# regenerated from the scripts, and too large for history
*.mp4

# the video3 review sheets and the toolbox's document preview: regenerated by
# run-video3.sh SHEET=1 / mktbox.py, and they carry software/archive/demo's supplied
# photograph, which is kept out of history (git 790de82)
software/archive/nitros9-video/build3/sheet-*.png
software/archive/nitros9-video/build3/tbox-doc.png

# ⚠ Blocktronics' "we-tortuga", the BBS scene's ANSI art, and the reference
# render its colours are checked against: NOT THIS PROJECT'S WORK, kept out of
# history the same way software/archive/demo's photograph is. Put the .ans in software/paint/reference/
# (it is in the 2016 "Block 'n' Roll" pack, on 16colo.rs) and v3show.have_art()
# puts the scene back; without it session3.py leaves those three commands out.
software/paint/reference/we-tortuga.ans
software/paint/reference/we-tortuga.ans.png
software/archive/nitros9-video/build3/sys/v3art

# ⛔ AND THE TABLE FILES GENERATED FROM THEM.  software/pcs/bench/mkpcs.py writes
# pcstbl/*.pbt out of the disk images above; they are the same shipped game
# data in the port's own container, and the container does not change who owns
# the bytes.  mkrom.sh copies whatever is there onto the card.
software/pcs/bench/pcstbl/
